Z||000000||TheFiero64||06-16-2000||11:31 AM||||I want to replace my fuel lines. Dose anyone know what size the line and fittings are?<BR>What is an AN size? <B>Jeg's catalog</B> lists them as an AN size. Thanks <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.||||reg||5
Z||000001||TOM||06-16-2000||08:34 PM||||Not sure but I dont know/think the stock fuel fittings are AN. I know the fuel pressure tap on the block is an AN-4||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||06-17-2000||01:13 AM||||Why not just go buy lines from the dealer?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||06-17-2000||09:33 AM||||AN stands for Army-Navy.  These fittings were designed for military hardware (aircraft) and have since made their way to the civilian and race car market.  If you look at any aircraft engine (military, or civilian these days) that is the only fittings you will ever see.  There are lots of "AN dress up" items available but they are just a fake cover, are a pain in the a$$ to mess with (some one put one on and old Jeep I had), don't help anything and are not cheap.  If you want the AN look and performance, get the real item. Not much more $ than the fake $hit.   ||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||06-17-2000||03:50 PM||||I have AN-10 lines to my fuel rail and AN-8 into the fuel pressure riser (not the stock unit, a vacuum control-module) I got them from Earls along with the right adapters for the input cupler on the fuel rail and at the fuel pump... the FMU came with adapters. total cost about $200 (not includding the pressure riser -$280-) and you can't really see it but you'll never have to worry about a leak again.. the AN are compression fitting.. the tighter you make then the better they seal.. BUT if you over do it since they are aluminum theyu WILL strip and you cannot tap them... unless your going for a show car or NHRA psycho car the stock ones will do just fine to well over 100psi!<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 06-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||06-17-2000||03:55 PM||||the schrader valve on the fuel rail is 4AN but thats not really usefull for much other then checking fuel pressure or using a NOS safety switch... you can't cram enough fuel through that tiny of a hole... AN6/8 will work fine on the car... mine was way over done.. cause i didn't know any better...  I know now i'll... and i'll just save my money. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||TOM||06-18-2000||01:53 AM||||I know it has no use but to check the fuel pressure and NO AN fittings are not compression fittings They are flare fittings. 37 degree to be exact not like a standaed 45 degree of auto fittings.||||reg||1
